speechtext:
There should have been a declaration that the situation was serious and emergent.
Mr. Gar son: I listened to my hon. friend with great interest, and I hope he will reciprocate. I would have thought that a perusal of the preamble of this bill by an eminent lawyer like the hon. member for Lake Centre would have disclosed that it was not based upon the declaration of an emergency or a state of apprehended war, but was being introduced for the reasons made very clear by the preamble. I think a reading of the preamble will make clear the constitutional basis for this statute; he who runs may read. It is as follows:
Whereas, the United Nations, of which Canada is a member, has found it necessary in the interests of world peace to take action to repel unprovoked aggression and to avert war;
And whereas the parliament of Canada has approved action by Canada, in discharge of its obligations under the charter of the United Nations, to assist in the measures taken by the United Nations;
And whereas effective action by Canada in order to safeguard the national security and to assist the United Nations in accordance with its obligations make it necessary to provide for defence preparations on a greatly increased scale;
That is a simple proposition. We have been going over it and emphasizing and discussing it. That is the very thing my hon. friends have been discussing so vehemently and at such great length in this session. The preamble continues:
And whereas steel and other materials and electrical energy and other services will be urgently required for such defence preparations and it is expected that the supply of such materials and services may be insufficient to meet such requirements together with the requirements of normal trade and commerce;
How many times have the members of my hon. friend's party been stressing the facts that are recited in this preamble? It goes on:
And whereas, in order to avert possible disruption of such defence preparations, to lessen the resultant disruption of normal trade and commerce and to prevent economic disorder and hardship on a national scale it is essential in the interest of Canada as a whole to provide for the control and regulation of the production, distribution and use of such materials and services . . .
In drafting this bill it was not in any sense necessary for us to think of infringing in 69262-33i
Essential Materials (Defence) Act any way upon those subject matters which are reserved to the legislative jurisdiction of the provinces. We are legislating fairly and squarely within the specific provisions of section 91 of the British North America Act, which set out clearly the powers of the dominion parliament, namely, trade and commerce and defence. That is the foundation of parliament's authority and that is the solution to-this great mystery that seems to puzzle my hon. friend as to why there is no recital of an emergency.
As I have said, if we had been anxious to take unto ourselves great powers under the present circumstances we could have done it under the other legislation. If We had attempted, as the hon. member seeks to insinuate, to invade the jurisdiction of the provinces, what simpler device could have been used than to declare there was a state of apprehended war? We have not done that. I think any reasonable person will agree that the measures taken are no more than are-necessary to deal with the serious situation with which we are confronted; and the powers, we are asking are much less than the powers which on the previous occasion we exercised with excellent judgment and excellent discretion, and which exercise of powers has been approved, not only by this parliament but by the people of Canada.
